<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Schussenried</h1><p class="st-meta">1440 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Bad Schussenried</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Premonstratensian abbey in upper Swabia, famed for its rococo library hall, whose abbots ruled a small imperial territory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Founded in 1183 by the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-schussenried">Schussenried</a> as a house of Premonstratensian canons, the community was raised to an abbey in 1440. Its abbots gradually freed themselves from outside advocates, gaining imperial immediacy and a seat among the Swabian prelates of the empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot ruled several villages in upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Swabia</a> as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-circle">Swabian Circle</a>, supported by farms, forests and tithes. The abbey commissioned the pilgrimage church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steinhausen-pilgrimage-church">Steinhausen</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominikus-zimmermann">Dominikus Zimmermann</a> from 1728, and in the 18th century began a grand new monastery whose library hall, painted by Franz Georg Hermann, is a masterpiece of rococo.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Secularised in 1803, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-schussenried">Schussenried</a> was granted to the counts of Sternberg-Manderscheid and passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a> in 1806. The library hall survives in Bad Schussenried.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-schussenried.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Schussenried" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mayer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schussenried_Kloster_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1440 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bad Schussenried</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schussenried Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1622&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-schussenried" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Schussenried on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4905 497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Schussenried lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5225 753L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5225 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1622&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-schussenried">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

